---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import BagCalculator from '../components/calculators/BagCalculator.astro';
import TopicClusterNav from '../components/TopicClusterNav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Quantity & Conversion', url: '/rock-coverage-calculator-yard' },
  { name: 'Rock Bag Calculator', url: '/rock-bag-calculator' }
];

const entityTags = [
  { label: 'Material', value: 'Bagged Landscape Rock' },
  { label: 'Count From', value: 'Labeled Cubic Feet' },
  { label: 'Compare', value: 'Local Bag and Bulk Quotes' }
];

const quickStats = [
  { value: '54', label: 'Half-cubic-foot bags per cubic yard', desc: '27 cu ft ÷ 0.5 cu ft; a volume conversion, not a weight rule' },
  { value: '2 sq ft', label: 'Coverage from a 0.5 cu ft bag', desc: 'At a 3-inch depth, before any extra allowance' },
  { value: 'Label first', label: 'Weight and pallet count', desc: 'Both vary with product and seller' }
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'How many 0.5 cubic foot bags of rock do I need for 100 square feet?',
    a: 'At a 3-inch finished depth, 100 square feet takes 25 cubic feet of rock. Divide by the bag’s 0.5-cubic-foot label to get 50 whole bags before any extra allowance. If you deliberately add 10% for site conditions, the plan becomes 27.5 cubic feet or 55 bags.'
  },
  {
    q: 'How many 50 lb bags of rock make one ton?',
    a: 'Forty 50 lb bags weigh one US short ton (2,000 lb). That weight conversion does not tell you their coverage: the volume inside a 50 lb bag depends on the particular rock. Read its cubic-foot volume or coverage label before counting bags for an area.'
  },
  {
    q: 'Is there a bag count where bulk rock is always cheaper?',
    a: 'No. Multiply your whole-bag count by the local bag price. Compare that with a bulk quote for the same type and sufficient volume, including minimum order, delivery and applicable fees. Access, handling and transport can change the practical choice.'
  },
  {
    q: 'How many rock bags can I carry in my vehicle?',
    a: 'Multiply the number of bags by their labeled weight, then use your vehicle’s tire and loading label to check the available occupants-and-cargo capacity. Passengers, tools and other cargo use that capacity too. A vehicle class name does not establish a safe bag count.'
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Rock Bag Calculator"
  description="Find whole bags of landscape rock from area, depth, and the volume printed on the bag. Estimate pallet split and weight from product labels, then compare local bag and bulk quotes."
  canonicalPath="/rock-bag-calculator"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "WebApplication",
      "name": "Rock Bag Calculator",
      "url": "https://rockcoveragecalculator.com/rock-bag-calculator",
      "description": "Calculates whole rock bags from area, depth and labeled bag volume, with optional labeled weight, pallet count and local cost comparison.",
      "applicationCategory": "UtilityApplication",
      "operatingSystem": "Web",
      "about": {
        "@type": "Thing",
        "name": "Retail Rock Bags"
      }
    })} />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "mainEntity": faqItems.map(item => ({
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": item.q,
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": item.a
        }
      }))
    })} />
  </Fragment>

  <Header />

  <main>
    <PageHero
      title="Rock Bag Calculator"
      highlightWord="Calculator"
      badge="Bagged Rock • Coverage & Buying"
      subtitle="A 100-square-foot bed at 3 inches needs 25 cubic feet of rock: 50 bags if each contains 0.5 cubic foot. Enter your product's labeled volume to get a whole-bag count, then check its weight and your local prices."
      breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
      entityTags={entityTags}
      quickStats={quickStats}
    />

    <BagCalculator />


    <!-- Technical Guide on Bag Calculations -->
    <article class="content-guide-section">
      <div class="container">
        <section class="content-card" id="bag-math">
          <h2>How to turn a rock layer into a bag count</h2>
          <p>First find the cubic feet needed to fill the space. Divide that volume by the <strong>cubic feet per bag</strong> on the product label, then round up to a whole bag. Enter any extra allowance before rounding. This works for decorative pebbles, pea gravel, lava rock, and crushed stone when the chosen package gives a usable volume.</p>
          <div class="formula-box">
            <code>Installed cu ft = area (sq ft) × depth (in) ÷ 12</code><br />
            <code>Planned cu ft = installed cu ft × (1 + extra % ÷ 100)</code><br />
            <code>Whole bags = round up (planned cu ft ÷ labeled cu ft per bag)</code>
          </div>
          <p>For a 10 × 10 ft bed at 3 inches, the installed volume is <strong>25 cu ft</strong> (about 0.93 yd³). With no extra allowance, that takes 100 quarter-cubic-foot bags, 50 half-cubic-foot bags, or 25 one-cubic-foot bags. A half-cubic-foot bag covers 2 sq ft at this depth.</p>
          <figure class="bag-figure">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 720 225" role="img" aria-labelledby="bag-figure-title bag-figure-desc" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
              <title id="bag-figure-title">One volume, three bag sizes</title>
              <desc id="bag-figure-desc">A 100-square-foot area at 3 inches deep needs 25 cubic feet. It takes 100 bags holding 0.25 cubic foot, 50 bags holding 0.5 cubic foot, or 25 bags holding 1 cubic foot.</desc>
              <rect x="2" y="2" width="716" height="221" rx="16" fill="#111c27" stroke="#42566b" />
              <text x="360" y="37" text-anchor="middle" fill="#f8fafc" font-size="21" font-weight="700">100 sq ft × 3 in ÷ 12 = 25 cu ft</text>
              <path d="M360 51v24M118 75h484" fill="none" stroke="#eab95c" stroke-width="2" />
              <path d="M118 75v15M360 75v15M602 75v15" fill="none" stroke="#eab95c" stroke-width="2" />
              <rect x="24" y="96" width="188" height="99" rx="12" fill="#24384a" />
              <rect x="266" y="96" width="188" height="99" rx="12" fill="#24384a" />
              <rect x="508" y="96" width="188" height="99" rx="12" fill="#24384a" />
              <text x="118" y="132" text-anchor="middle" fill="#f8fafc" font-size="17">0.25 cu ft/bag</text>
              <text x="360" y="132" text-anchor="middle" fill="#f8fafc" font-size="17">0.5 cu ft/bag</text>
              <text x="602" y="132" text-anchor="middle" fill="#f8fafc" font-size="17">1 cu ft/bag</text>
              <text x="118" y="174" text-anchor="middle" fill="#eab95c" font-size="30" font-weight="700">100 bags</text>
              <text x="360" y="174" text-anchor="middle" fill="#eab95c" font-size="30" font-weight="700">50 bags</text>
              <text x="602" y="174" text-anchor="middle" fill="#eab95c" font-size="30" font-weight="700">25 bags</text>
            </svg>
            <figcaption>Equal installed volume, different package counts. Check the volume of the actual bag you plan to buy.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <h3>Why a cubic yard is 54 half-cubic-foot bags</h3>
          <p>One cubic yard contains 27 cubic feet, so <strong>27 ÷ 0.5 = 54 bags</strong> by volume. For a one-cubic-foot bag, the same yard is 27 bags. This is a package-volume conversion; it does not say how much a cubic yard weighs or how many bags are on a pallet. Use the <a href="/rock-coverage-calculator-yard">cubic yard calculator</a> when a yardage estimate is your starting point.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="content-card" id="bag-label">
          <h2>What if the rock bag is labeled only in pounds?</h2>
          <p>Pounds describe carrying weight, while cubic feet describe the volume available to cover the bed. They cannot be swapped without the product's loose density. Retail listings make the distinction clear: <a href="https://www.homedepot.com/p/302985371" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Vigoro Creek Rock</a> lists 0.5 cu ft and 40 lb; <a href="https://www.homedepot.com/p/100558618" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Vigoro River Pebble</a> lists 0.5 cu ft and 50 lb. The same package volume can have a different weight.</p>
          <p>If the front of a bag says only “50 lb,” look for the manufacturer's cubic-foot yield or coverage at a stated depth. If neither appears, ask the seller before using the calculator. Forty 50 lb bags weigh one US short ton, but that fact alone does not establish their coverage. <a href="https://store.astm.org/c0029_c0029m-23.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASTM C29/C29M</a> describes how aggregate bulk density is measured; it does not provide one universal pounds-to-volume value.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="content-card" id="bag-order">
          <h2>Turn the count into a workable pickup or delivery plan</h2>
          <ol class="bag-checklist">
            <li><strong>Match the material.</strong> Check rock type, size range, and whether it contains fines. A similarly named bulk product may not match the bagged one.</li>
            <li><strong>Check each pallet listing.</strong> Enter the seller's stated bags per pallet; pallet quantities vary. Ask whether a mixed order of full pallets and loose bags is available.</li>
            <li><strong>Check your vehicle's actual limit.</strong> Multiply whole bags by labeled weight, then account for passengers, tools, and other cargo. <a href="https://www.nhtsa.gov/vehicle-safety/tires" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NHTSA's tire guidance</a> explains the tire and loading label. A “half-ton pickup” name is not a payload rating.</li>
            <li><strong>Compare complete prices.</strong> There is no dependable bag-count cutoff for bulk. Compare the bag bill with a delivered quote for enough of the same rock, including the supplier's minimum and fees. Allow for the difference in access and labor.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>Need a material-specific route? Use the <a href="/crushed-stone-bag-calculator">crushed stone bag calculator</a> for a base or repair, the <a href="/river-rock-bag-calculator">river rock bag calculator</a> for rounded stone, or the <a href="/pea-gravel-bag-calculator">pea gravel bag calculator</a> for smaller rounded gravel. The <a href="/bulk-vs-bagged-rock">bulk versus bagged rock guide</a> covers ordering tradeoffs.</p>
        </section>

        <!-- FAQ Section -->
        <div class="content-card faq-card">
          <h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Bagged Rock</h2>
          <div class="faq-accordion">
            {faqItems.map((item, index) => (
              <details class="faq-detail" open={index === 0}>
                <summary class="faq-summary">
                  <span>{item.q}</span>
                  <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg>
                </summary>
                <div class="faq-answer">
                  <p>{item.a}</p>
                </div>
              </details>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>

    <TopicClusterNav currentPage="/rock-bag-calculator" category="quantity" />
  </main>

  <Footer />
  <script src="../scripts/app.js"></script>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .content-guide-section {
    padding: var(--space-12) 0;
    background: var(--bg-body);
  }

  .content-card {
    background: var(--bg-card);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    padding: var(--space-8);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-8);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  .content-card h2 {
    font-size: var(--text-2xl);
    color: var(--text-primary);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }

  .content-card h3 {
    font-size: var(--text-xl);
    color: var(--text-primary);
    margin: var(--space-6) 0 var(--space-3) 0;
  }

  .formula-box {
    background: var(--bg-surface);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-gold);
    padding: var(--space-4);
    border-radius: 0 var(--radius-md) var(--radius-md) 0;
    margin: var(--space-4) 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    color: var(--accent-gold);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  .bag-figure {
    margin: var(--space-6) 0;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .bag-figure svg {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 580px;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
  }

  .bag-figure figcaption {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
  }

  .bag-checklist {
    padding-left: 1.4rem;
  }

  .bag-checklist li {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
  }

  .content-card a {
    color: var(--accent-gold);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }

  .table-wrapper {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin: var(--space-6) 0;
  }

  .guide-table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    text-align: left;
  }

  .guide-table th, .guide-table td {
    padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-4);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
  }

  .guide-table th {
    background: var(--bg-surface);
    color: var(--text-primary);
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .guide-table tr:hover td {
    background: rgba(212, 168, 83, 0.04);
  }

  .faq-detail {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
    padding: var(--space-4) 0;
  }

  .faq-summary {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: center;
    cursor: pointer;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text-primary);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    list-style: none;
  }

  .faq-summary::-webkit-details-marker {
    display: none;
  }

  .faq-summary svg {
    transition: transform var(--transition-fast);
  }

  .faq-detail[open] .faq-summary svg {
    transform: rotate(180deg);
  }

  .faq-answer {
    padding-top: var(--space-3);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
</style>
